Glacial Grit

Creative Catalyst: Isolated lighthouse keepers once modulated their beacon frequencies to transmit rhythmic codes to passing trawlers during the endless polar night.

Glacial Grit is a cold-blooded collision of hyper-kinetic neurofunk percussion and the hook-driven euphoria of dance pop, rendered through a stark, brutalist sonic lens. It sounds like a high-speed chase through a frozen shipyard; the low-end is serrated and industrial, while the high-end is dominated by the shimmering, metallic microtonal wail of the electric sitar. It is music for the architectural fringe—unapologetically rhythmic, physically punishing, and hauntingly melodic.

Genre DNA
* Tempo & Pulse: 172-175 BPM; utilizes "halftime-swing" neurofunk drumming that feels like a machine heart skipping beats.
* Harmonic Palette: Phrygian and Locrian modes, favoring dissonant, industrial intervals that resolve into bright, saccharine dance-pop hooks.
* Production Signatures: Excessive use of granular synthesis on organic sitar recordings; "concrete-reverb" (short-decay reflections mimicking large metal silos); side-chain compression so aggressive the entire mix "inhales" and "exhales" with the kick drum.
* Dynamics: Sudden, jarring drops into skeletal, brittle passages followed by overwhelming walls of industrial sound.

Scene & Origin Lore
The scene emerged in the mid-90s on the Svalbard archipelago, where bored lighthouse technicians began cross-referencing their signal-transmitting gear with shortwave radio pirate broadcasts. They wore heavy-duty, orange rubberized oilskins paired with bespoke, modular flight gear—aesthetic precursors to modern tech-wear. Their rituals involved "Signal-Locking," where the audience would stand motionless in semi-dark, concrete-bunker clubs, waiting for the sitar’s buzzing resonance to cut through the distorted bass, a sonic surrogate for the lighthouse beams that once guided their ancestors through the frost.

Instruments: Electric Sitar (processed through distortion), modular Eurorack bass oscillators, granular samplers, and heavily mangled 90s digital drum kits.

For Fans Of: Venetian Snares, SOPHIE, Squarepusher, Anoushka Shankar (experimental phase).

Fictional Band Name: The Keeper’s Lattice.

Band Description: A trio consisting of an ex-radio engineer, a structural welder, and a Hindustani-trained session guitarist. They perform on a stage constructed entirely of rusted scaffolding, wearing pressurized suits that hiss with pneumatic venting during crescendos. Their style is calculated chaos—the guitarist plays with surgical precision while the engineer manipulates live feedback loops from local maritime radio traffic.

